every morning on the train and over and over again in public spaces where I'd be out and about, I would see people standing cheek to jaw well next to each other or sitting cheek to jaw well next to each other and ignoring each other. Nick is a behavioral science professor at the University of Chicago Boots School of Business. He started thinking about this odd behavior. Why do we ignore one another? One possibility is that strangers in fact are unpleasant. We don't know them and striking up a conversation would be less pleasant than sitting there in solitude so people are maximizing their well-being by sitting on the train or maybe in a cab or on a
每天早上在火车上，以及在公共场所，我一次又一次地看到人们脸贴着脸站在彼此旁边，或者脸贴着脸坐在彼此旁边，却无视对方。尼克是芝加哥大学布斯商学院的行为科学教授。他开始思考这种奇怪的行为。为什么我们无视彼此？一种可能性是陌生人实际上是不愉快的。我们不认识他们，开始交谈会比坐在那里孤独地待着更不愉快，所以人们通过坐在火车上，或者可能在出租车里，或者在

70
