bus or in a waiting room and ignoring each other. That's possible. The other possibility is that we actually underestimate the benefits that come from social engagement. That we think we'd be happier sitting in solitude and in fact we're just wrong about that. Nick and his co-author, Julianna Schroder, decided to figure out what was going on. They ran an experiment that involved commuters. Volunteers were assigned to one of three conditions, engage with the people around them, sit in solitude, or act as they normally would on their commute. People predicted that they would be the least happy if they had to engage with other people on the train. They were sure that what would make them happy was to be simply left alone.
公共汽车或候诊室里无视彼此来最大化他们的幸福感。这有可能。另一种可能性是我们实际上低估了社会参与带来的好处。我们认为坐在孤独中会更快乐，而事实上我们对此是错误的。尼克和他的合著者朱莉安娜·施罗德（Julianna Schroder）决定弄清楚发生了什么。他们进行了一项涉及通勤者的实验。志愿者被分配到三种情况之一：与周围的人互动，独自坐着，或者像往常一样通勤。人们预测，如果他们必须在火车上与其他人互动，他们会最不快乐。他们确信能让他们快乐的是简单地被单独留下。

71
00:40:45.000 --> 00:41:12.000
But that's not what the study found. People reported being happier, less sad, and having a more pleasant commute when they connected with the person sitting next to them, than when we randomly assigned them to a condition where they were asked to sit in solitude. People's perception that their fellow commuters wanted to be left alone didn't match reality. What was happening was that volunteers thought People's perception that their fellow commuters wanted to be left alone didn't match reality. What was happening was that volunteers thought that others were less interested in talking to them than they actually were.
